{"name":"Sydney","slug":"sydney","region":"Southeast Asia & Oceania","lat":-33.87,"lon":151.21,"seasons":{"DJF":{"season_name":"Dec 2026–Feb 2027","years":[1951,1952,1953,1954,1955,1956,1957,1958,1959,1960,1961,1962,1963,1964,1965,1966,1967,1968,1969,1970,1971,1972,1973,1974,1975,1976,1977,1978,1979,1980,1981,1982,1983,1984,1985,1986,1987,1988,1989,1990,1991,1992,1993,1994,1995,1996,1997,1998,1999,2000,2001,2002,2003,2004,2005,2006,2007,2008,2009,2010,2011,2012,2013,2014,2015,2016,2017,2018,2019,2020,2021,2022,2023,2024,2025],"roni_lead":[-0.38,0.98,0.04,0.84,-0.31,-1.31,-0.14,1.16,0.24,0.04,0.08,-0.16,-0.3,1.06,-0.64,1.86,-0.03,-0.08,0.56,0.67,-0.57,-0.4,1.82,-1.63,-0.45,-0.95,1.04,0.89,-0.08,0.52,-0.06,-0.17,2.03,-0.9,-0.61,-0.18,1.0,1.2,-1.71,-0.23,0.27,1.2,0.1,0.46,0.97,-0.93,-0.25,2.22,-1.33,-1.06,-0.55,-0.18,1.18,0.17,0.7,-0.18,0.73,-1.33,-0.38,0.86,-1.61,-0.91,0.21,-0.22,0.35,2.03,-0.96,-0.82,0.62,0.15,-1.37,-1.08,-1.1,1.3,-0.76],"paragraph":"Dec–Feb in Sydney has warmed by about 0.2 °C per decade since 1951, roughly 1.5 °C in all. There is a strong, well-established El Niño effect on temperature: after El Niño conditions in the preceding Sep–Nov, Dec–Feb tends to run warmer, and El Niño accounts for about 15% of the year-to-year ups and downs. With this year's strong El Niño, that push is about 70% of a normal year-to-year swing, roughly as much warmth as 27 years of the long-term trend. Without El Niño, a Dec–Feb this warm would come about once every 4 years. El Niño moves the hottest days most: this season they are expected to shift by about 1.3 °C, against 0.4 °C for the coldest days. Humidity shows no clear long-term trend, but El Niño has a strong, well-established effect toward a less humid Dec–Feb, and this year's shift is substantial. Rainfall shows no clear trend or El Niño link. For Dec 2026–Feb 2027, a warmer-than-normal season is likely (about 70% chance), about 0.9 °C above the 1991–2020 average; a less humid season than normal is more likely than not (about 55%). The El Niño expected for Sep–Nov 2026 is stronger than any in the record used here, so the outlook extends the historical relationship beyond what has been observed. This outlook is based on the El Niño strength expected for Sep–Nov 2026 and will be updated when it is observed in early December.","variables":{"temperature":{"normal_1991_2020":22.4504,"trend_per_decade":0.1969,"trend_p":0.0,"trend_total_since_1951":1.4766,"enso_effect_per_degC":0.2949,"enso_p":0.0019,"confidence":"Strong","survives_fdr":true,"fraction_explained":0.1266,"intrinsic_sd":0.726,"effect_this_season":0.53,"effect_ratio":0.7301,"size":"Substantial","size_typical_el_nino":"Noticeable","once_every_n_years_without_el_nino":4,"expected_anomaly_2027":0.9097,"forecast_mean":23.3601,"forecast_sd":0.7768,"p_below":0.0524,"p_near":0.2286,"p_above":0.719,"forecast_words":"Likely (72%): a warmer-than-normal season","within_record":false,"enso_effect_shrunk_per_degC":0.2095,"shrink_factor":0.7103,"effect_this_season_raw":0.7461,"trend_part_2027":0.3797,"roni_upper_within_record":false,"years_of_warming_equivalent":26.9194,"percentiles":{"p05":{"trend_per_decade":0.1388,"trend_p":0.0052,"enso_effect_per_degC":0.1723,"enso_p":0.1287,"confidence":"No identifiable effect","shift_this_season":0.436,"lines_cross":false},"p50":{"trend_per_decade":0.2289,"trend_p":0.0,"enso_effect_per_degC":0.2527,"enso_p":0.0008,"confidence":"Strong","shift_this_season":0.6393,"lines_cross":false},"p95":{"trend_per_decade":0.215,"trend_p":0.0025,"enso_effect_per_degC":0.5274,"enso_p":0.0001,"confidence":"Strong","shift_this_season":1.3344,"lines_cross":false},"tails":{"enso_difference":0.3551,"enso_difference_p":0.0136,"trend_difference":0.0762,"trend_difference_p":0.2433}},"values":[20.951,22.13,20.645,22.175,22.295,21.451,21.757,21.944,21.332,22.364,20.81,21.154,21.162,21.906,20.761,21.579,21.493,21.832,21.73,21.599,21.637,20.698,22.758,21.631,21.54,21.29,22.763,22.623,22.328,22.878,23.075,22.32,23.05,21.